    "result_text": "The build is green. The test `coreCountsNamesakes` passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` feature for `POST /api/owners`, following the existing OfficeFloor function-injection patterns used for the other derived/persisted owner fields (`customerCode`, `householdId`).\n\n**What `namesakeCount` means:** at create time (before the new owner is persisted), it's the number of *existing* owners sharing the same `firstName` **and** `lastName`, compared case-insensitively. It's computed once, stored, and returned on subsequent reads.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer (`format: int32`, `minimum: 0`) to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `namesake_count` column (`Integer namesakeCount`) with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it to/from the DTO by name automatically.\n\n3. **DB schema** \u2014 added `namesake_count INTEGER` to the owners table in `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` (used by the test suite) and `db/h2/schema.sql` (used by the running app). These are the schemas that already carry `customer_code`/`household_id`.\n\n4. **`AssignNamesakeCount.java`** (new function) \u2014 iterates `ownerRepository.findAll()` (which excludes the not-yet-saved new owner) and counts owners matching both names case-insensitively, mutating the entity in place.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `assignNamesakeCount` step (under `transaction` governance) between `assignHousehold` and `save`, so the count is set before the owner is persisted.\n\n`GET /api/owners/{id}` already responds via `OwnerMapper.toOwnerDto`, so the stored value is returned without further changes.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
